Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Orchid Roots Choke the Tapehead
- Story & Context: At the height of the pre-dawn market rush at Bang Phlat Canal, Ma Nirmala jammed an illegal outboard motor battery into her master tape deck, unleashing a 70-BPM sub-bass tremor that shook ripe jackfruit from the overhanging trees straight into the water. The excessive current caught a tangle of living orchid vines on fire, yet she refused to cut the master fader, letting the crackling flora and burning resin bleed directly into the open microphone while vendors cheered from their skiffs. The track captures that exact morning accident, preserved beneath the warble of charred tape oxide as she chanted through a brass megaphone, "Cool water take the fire, but let the heavy drop roll."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Album cover art, 35mm film still. A vintage magnetic reel-to-reel tape deck overgrown with damp tropical green vines and blooming purple orchid roots inside the belly of a wooden rowboat. Early morning lilac light filtering through canal mist, kerosene smoke, amber VU meters glowing, macro details of water droplets on magnetic tape.

Track 2: Nirmala’s Floating Glasshouse
- Story & Context: Moored deep inside the Nakhon Silt Basin during an unannounced municipal tax raid, Didi Somying shielded three dozen illicit produce barges by blasting an impossibly low, vibrating bayan glissando that created localized standing waves, stranding the patrol skiffs on a shallow mudbank. With her fingers stained dark purple from crushing wild elderberry sap into her mixing faders, she leaned into an antique ribbon mic to whisper a confession of fifty years spent smuggling Jamaican vinyl records inside hollowed-out gourds. Her voice drifts in and out of the warm Rhodes skank like morning river vapor, softly murmuring the refrain, "I bury every secret right between the drum and the damp."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric lyric video background. An interior view of a floating conservatory made of weathered glass frames and green timber slats adrift on a slow canal at sunrise. Giant tropical philodendron leaves, potted ferns, dust motes dancing in warm honey-colored sunbeams, an old upright piano draped in wet burlap, soft golden haze.

Track 3: "Will the Graft Hold Until Sunrise?"
- Story & Context: Legend says that after the catastrophic monsoon flood of the Lotus Sluice drowned every wooden dock, the matriarchs tied their boats to a half-submerged banyan tree and performed a fourteen-minute drone piece to keep the drowned seeds from rotting in the riverbed. Auntie Parvati used a damp clay pestle to grind the skin of her copper drum, generating a subterranean friction whine so pure it coaxed silver river carp to jump straight into the empty vegetable crates. Recorded on a single battery-powered boombox balanced on a bamboo raft, the aftermath captured the grandmothers laughing quietly as the waters receded, quietly harmonizing the line, "The river swallowed our stalls, but our roots still shake the silt."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Surreal, dreamy wide-angle photograph. A solitary colossal banyan tree rising from deep green canal waters at sunrise, its sprawling aerial roots wrapped around submerged vintage loudspeakers and wooden fruit crates. Soft peach and teal greenhouse sky, silver fish leaping out of calm mirrored water, misty and tranquil aftermath aesthetic.

